      <groundspeak:long_description html="True"> </groundspeak:long_description>
      <groundspeak:encoded_hints>IRON PIPE 15 CM AGL .3 M SE GP

IRON PIPE WITH BRASS CAP 15 CM AGL, 30 CM SE OF A GP.
27.4 M WEST OF THE C/L OF A FORESTRYROAD; 2.5 M BELOW ROAD LEVEL;

elevation 1401.3m</groundspeak:encoded_hints>
      <groundspeak:logs>
        <groundspeak:log id="694914438">
          <groundspeak:date>2017-06-16T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="836983">Coolcat21</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP313 - Cabin Creek

Posted: N53° 45.386' W118° 21.046'

My GPSr: N53° 45.389' W118° 21.047'

(FOUND)&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;I parked just off the highway at N53° 45.441' W118° 20.720' and made my way - just over 300m as I remember - along what is now a quad trail to the cap. I'm pretty sure I got this idea for a parking spot from van der Decken's find log and Google Street View confirmed it looked great.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The prominent &quot;Bridge Out&quot; sign was amusing. &quot;What bridge?&quot; says the non-local. I'm thinking that another &quot;Bridge Out Sign is Out&quot; sign might be in order.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;From there it was a straight-forward stroll to the cap side along the quad trail. And I think this is the first time I've seen a prominent &quot;BM&quot; sign at a cap. It made me reluctant to touch it, if you know what I mean. 🙂&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Thanks for the BM!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="235670374">
          <groundspeak:date>2012-05-17T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="664594">Sears Tower</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP313 - Cabin Creek&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We were in Hinton for work, and decided to get a few more caps.  This was the furthest north for this trip.  After walking to the cap, I realized I left my phone in the truck so I will be sending the numbers to you!  TFTH&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="135163063">
          <groundspeak:date>2010-11-08T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1565005">Takin it Easy</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP313 Cabin Creek 

Posted N53° 45.386 W118° 21.046 

GPSr N53° 45.385 W118° 21.048&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Another fine example of following vdD's directions right to the cap.  I approached from the north and quickly spotted vdD's culvert and trail.  Given the failing light, I was really appreciative of the info as I would likely have bushwhacked my way in.  Another cap in the middle of nowhere and that's likely why it is in pristine shape (that and how high it is AGL).  Thanks for the cap OFTH!&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;This entry was edited by Takin it Easy on Thursday, 11 November 2010 at 20:09:11.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="81032908">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="53.756467" lon="-118.350783" />
          <groundspeak:date>2009-08-15T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="1074559">van der Decken</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP313 - Cabin Creek

Elev. 1401 metres&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Two visits in as many days after a three year absence of visitors? Shrug! Who can figure out cappers! &lt;img src="/images/icons/icon_smile_wink.gif" border="0" align="middle" /&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;The parking trick for me was to come at things from the north after picking up a cache on the north side of the Berland river. As I was coming up the hill, I could see a culvert underneath the old road alignment, and it's possibly to pull completely off onto the shoulder there. It's a nice easy downhill stroll, with only a slight gradient (which means you won't be huffing and puffing on the return trip. The old road seems to be used by the locals as a quad trail, so it's easier to follow the trail to within 20 metres of the cap than bushwhacking the direct route.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As PDOP's noted 3 years ago, there's no view to speak of from the cap. There's plenty of flagging in the bushes and trees around the cap, so even if the cap wasn't 6 inches above ground level and marked with an MP you'd still have little trouble finding it. The cap is in excellent shape and didn't even need to be cleaned. That surprised me until I discovered that Sleepy Hollow had just logged it yesterday! Thanks for the cap, outforthehunt.&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
        <groundspeak:log id="80736379">
          <groundspeak:log_wpt lat="53.756433" lon="-118.350767" />
          <groundspeak:date>2009-08-14T20:00:00</groundspeak:date>
          <groundspeak:type>Found it</groundspeak:type>
          <groundspeak:finder id="321730">Sleepy_hollow</groundspeak:finder>
          <groundspeak:text encoded="True">&lt;p&gt;BCP313 - Cabin Creek&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;We enjoyed the quiet walk down the old roadway to the cap site. What a pristine cap and who would ever think to look for a cap in this location. Great stuff - just a fine location - TFTH and fun - the picture is on the way!&lt;/p&gt;

          </groundspeak:text>
        </groundspeak:log>
      </groundspeak:logs>
      <groundspeak:travelbugs />
    </groundspeak:cache>
